  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Typical Summertime pattern continues with building \nheat next week. Storm chances today decreasing Sunday and Monday.\n\nIt's a typical Summertime pattern as we enter the second weekend and \nweek of August. The forecast is on repeat with patchy morning dense \nfog giving way to hot and humid conditions with late day afternoon \nand evening thunderstorms. \n\nToday, we'll bring another passing shortwave disturbance which will \ntrigger an additional round of afternoon and evening strong \nthunderstorms. Thunderstorm coverage will be a bit less due to mid \nlevel west to west/west-southwest flow yielding some downsloping \nflow east of the mountains. This is a stark contrast to the \nsoutherly flow over the past few days yielding locally higher storm \ncoverage. \n\nStorms today will be isolated to scattered with a focus again along \nand north of I-66/US-50 and east of the Allegheny Mountains.\nInstability will remain high as a result of the high heat and \nhumidity. Shear values will sit around 30 to 35 kts as the \nsubtropical jet stream begins to strengthen. This could yield a more \norganized severe weather threat which aligns with current learning \nmachine guidance that suggest 15-30 percent probabilities for \ndamaging wind gusts this afternoon and evening. Thermodynamic \nprofiles also depict this sentiment with skinnier CAPE profiles and \ninverted-V signatures towards the surface yielding a higher ceiling \nfor damaging wind potential. The Storm Prediction Center continues a \nMarginal Risk for severe weather (Level 1 out of 5) for this \nafternoon and evening with wind as the primary threat. Isolated \ninstances of flooding also cannot be ruled out, especially over the \nurban areas around Baltimore/DC given the repetitive heavy rains the \nlast few days. \n\nConvection wanes a bit Sunday into Monday given quasi-zonal flow \naloft and the strengthen upper level ridge axis nearby. A few storms \nmay develop over the higher elevations, but coverage will be much \nless than in recent days. This will be short-lived however, with \nstorm chances increasing once again Tuesday through the end of the \nweek as a series of shortwave disturbances pass through. \n\nOutside of he storm chances expect highs in the upper 80s and low \n90s with heat indices east of the Blue Ridge up around 100 degrees. \nThe hottest days look to be Monday and Tuesday given the \nstrengthened subtropical ridge over the region. Highs will push into \nthe mid 90s with heat indices closer to 105 degrees east of the Blue \nRidge/along the urban corridor.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A cold front could arrive late next week bringing \nrelief from the heat and humidity into the upcoming weekend. \n\nDaily storm chances look to continue through at least Friday as a \nmore potent frontal system tracks toward the region. Ahead of that \nfront will be several fast moving shortwave disturbances which will \nhelp aid in shower and thunderstorm development during the diurnal \nperiod. Storm coverage is a bit uncertain with activity remaining \nisolated to scattered Tuesday becoming a scattered to numerous \nWednesday into late week as the front tries to push through. \nSpeaking of the front, details remain a bit murky on it's passage. \nMost 00z ensemble/deterministic guidance suggests a frontal passage \nThursday afternoon into Saturday. Guidance also depicts an MCS type \nsetup during the Tuesday and Wednesday timeframe. Machine learning \nguidance is grabbing onto this as well with probabilities running \nbetween 15 to 30 percent for severe weather during the midweek \ntimeframe. SPC mentions areas back toward our west on the Day 4 and \n5 outlook, so something we'll continue to monitor, especially as the \nsubtropical ridge flattens. \n\nThere is a bit more uncertainty in regards to severe weather \nThursday through Saturday. Of course with any decent frontal passage \nwill have to be monitor given machine probabilities remaining \nbetween 5-30 percent for severe weather. This will ultimately be \ndetermined by the timing of the front in regards to the peak heating \nperiod. Temperatures will cool from the 90s into the 80s late week. \nWeather does look to improve for the upcoming weekend as Canadian \nhigh pressure works into the region.",
